WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.500
A droid-filled city features prominently&nbsp;
in the new episode of "The Mandalorian,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.800
and one of those background bots&nbsp;
even had a bit part in "The Empire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:12.120
Strikes Back." Here's the throwback&nbsp;
robot cameo you may have missed.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:16.200
Season 3 of "The Mandalorian" picks&nbsp;
up where we last left off — that is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:20.640
provided you've watched the Din Djarin bits&nbsp;
of "The Book of Boba Fett" and know what he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.500
and Grogu have been up to. As is the series'&nbsp;
custom, it also features numerous nods at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:29.760
the many canon and non-canon stories from&nbsp;
the history of the Galaxy Far, Far Away.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:34.440
In other words, the Season 3 premiere&nbsp;
features lots of cool droids. The most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:39.780
prominent of them is, of course, IG-11, whose&nbsp;
self-destruction sequence in Season 1 apparently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:44.460
wasn't enough to entirely demolish his body,&nbsp;
and whom Mando is now trying to get revived&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:48.840
for the sake of having some reliable-ish&nbsp;
backup on his upcoming Mandalore jaunt.

00:00:49.085 --> 00:00:50.405
"I need him back."

00:00:52.881 --> 00:00:56.961
"IG-11 was destroyed on the lava&nbsp;
river. This is just a statue."

00:00:56.961 --> 00:01:01.140
However, the episode's backdrop of&nbsp;
the surprisingly spruced-up Nevarro&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:06.120
City hides plenty of other mechanical marvels&nbsp;
to behold, from the tiny droids hauling Greef&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:11.280
Karga's cape to various protocol droids. In&nbsp;
fact, one of these cameos just so happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:16.200
to mark the unexpected return of a long-lost&nbsp;
deep cut character from the "Star Wars" lore.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:20.400
Early on in the episode, Din and Grogu&nbsp;
are walking the streets of the city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:23.248
taking in the sights and&nbsp;
marveling at the overhaul.

00:01:23.248 --> 00:01:25.588
"A lot has changed around here."

00:01:25.588 --> 00:01:29.040
On their way, they briefly pass&nbsp;
a bright white protocol droid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.180
that a few hardcore fans will&nbsp;
no doubt recognize as K-3PO.

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:37.680
This white-colored droid resembling&nbsp;
C-3PO first appeared in "Star Wars:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:43.140
Episode 5 — The Empire Strikes Back" as a member&nbsp;
of the Rebels, but he's mainly been a niche&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:48.060
fixture of the "Star Wars" canon since then. Based&nbsp;
on this brief glimpse, it's rather likely that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:52.860
monochrome droid is not actually the exact same&nbsp;
character fans first witnessed fighting the good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:57.660
fight in "The Empire Strikes Back," but the visual&nbsp;
design of the model is still quite unmistakable.

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:03.060
Apart from his original movie debut, K-3PO&nbsp;
has popped up in a few other entries in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:06.480
the franchise. He's gotten some cameos&nbsp;
in the "Star Wars Battlefront" games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:10.860
featured in a small role for several of the&nbsp;
"LEGO Star Wars" games, and has appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:15.480
in a couple other pieces of secondary and&nbsp;
non-canon franchise material. Nonetheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:19.860
"The Mandalorian" marks the oft-forgotten&nbsp;
droid's highest-profile appearance in years.

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:24.300
While the K-3PO droid unit that appears in&nbsp;
the Season 3 premiere of "The Mandalorian"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:28.020
may not be the same one that originally&nbsp;
appeared in "The Empire Strikes Back,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:31.800
some fans may nonetheless be&nbsp;
curious about the role that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:36.190
character originally played in the movie.&nbsp;
Suffice to say, it's not a very big one.

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:41.700
Like his counterpart on "The Mandalorian," K-3PO&nbsp;
is mostly resigned to being a background character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:45.960
in several scenes. He's seen working with the&nbsp;
Rebels' equipment at their secret base on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:50.760
icy planet Hoth, and he's present when the&nbsp;
iconic Battle of Hoth breaks out. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:54.360
this epic "Star Wars" battle doesn't&nbsp;
really go all that well for the Rebels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:58.966
and K-3PO is summarily destroyed in the&nbsp;
chaos during the planet's evacuation.

00:02:58.966 --> 00:03:04.020
Considering that the K-3PO on "The Mandalorian"&nbsp;
is in perfect condition, and the show takes place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:08.040
years after "The Empire Strikes Back," it's&nbsp;
probably not the same one from the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:13.920
movie. However, destroyed droids like IG-11 have&nbsp;
been brought back to life before, so there's still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:18.420
a possibility these two units are one and the&nbsp;
same. If "The Mandalorian" ever elects to give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:23.771
this smaller "Star Wars" character a bigger role,&nbsp;
perhaps fans will finally get a definitive answer.

